Q: BullMQ and NestJS - Manage Queue Names A: <p>The root cause is a circular dependency / module resolution timing issue.</p>
<p><code>@InjectQueue('email-queue')</code> internally generates an injection token like <code>BullQueue_email-queue</code>. If the constant is defined inside the service or module file itself, the token can mismatch or fail to resolve at the right time during NestJS bootstrapping.</p>
<p>The fix is to define your queue name constants in a dedicated constants file that neither the module nor the service owns — both import from it.</p>
<p>Step 1 — Create a constants file</p>
<pre><code>// src/queues/queues.constants.ts
export const QUEUE_NAMES = {
  EMAIL: 'email-queue',
  NOTIFICATIONS: 'notification-queue',
  // add more queues here as you grow
} as const;
</code></pre>
<p>Step 2 — Use it in the module</p>
<pre><code>import { QUEUE_NAMES } from '../queues/queues.constants';

@Module({
  imports: [
    BullModule.registerQueueAsync({
      name: QUEUE_NAMES.EMAIL,
    }),
  ],
  controllers: [EmailController],
  providers: [EmailQueueService],
  exports: [EmailQueueService],
})
export class EmailModule {}
</code></pre>
<p>Step 3 — Use it in the service</p>
<pre><code>import { InjectQueue } from '@nestjs/bullmq';
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { Queue } from 'bullmq';
import { QUEUE_NAMES } from '../queues/queues.constants';

@Injectable()
export class EmailQueueService {
  constructor(
    @InjectQueue(QUEUE_NAMES.EMAIL) private queue: Queue,
  ) {}

  async addMessage(dto: any) {
    return this.queue.add('send-message', dto);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Why your original approach likely failed — the constant was probably defined inside the service or module file, causing one of these issues:</p>
<p>The constant file created a circular import between the module and the service.
The constant value wasn't available at decorator evaluation time during NestJS's metadata reflection phase.</p>
<p>You were importing from <code>@nestjs/bull</code> but using <code>@nestjs/bullmq</code> (or vice versa) — these are different packages with different injection token prefixes.</p>
<p>Also make sure <code>BullModule.forRoot()</code> (or <code>forRootAsync()</code>) is registered in your root <strong>AppModule</strong> with the Redis connection config, not just in the feature module.</p>
<p>I wrote a detailed breakdown here:
<a href="https://www.arihantjain.cv/blogs/dynamic-notification-engine-bullmq-redis" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.arihantjain.cv/blogs/dynamic-notification-engine-bullmq-redis</a></p>

Q: Is it possible to have it automatically put links around header elements? A: <p>This doesn't seem to be possible out of the box. There are two ways of working around it that I can think of: using JavaScript, as revelt suggested, or using HTML in your markdown.</p>
<p>For example, consider the HTML you supplied above.</p>

<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;a href=&quot;/post/cool-blog-post#some-interesting-title&quot;&gt;
    &lt;h2 id=&quot;some-interesting-title&quot;&gt;Some Interesting Title&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you put this code directly into a Hugo markdown document, it will produce the kind of link you are looking for. However, this is a pain to type every time, so to reduce your work you can make a <a href="https://gohugo.io/extras/shortcodes/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">shortcode</a>.</p>
<p>In <code>layouts/shortcodes/link-heading.html</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>{{ $id := .Get 0 | lower | replaceRE &quot;[^0-9a-z]&quot; &quot;-&quot; | replaceRE &quot;-+&quot; &quot;-&quot; -}}
&lt;a href=&quot;#{{ $id }}&quot;&gt;
  &lt;h2 id=&quot;{{ $id }}&quot;&gt;{{ .Get 0 }}&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In your markdown document:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>{{&lt; link-heading &quot;Some Interesting Title&quot; &gt;}}
</code></pre>
<p>I have left the base URL out here, but you can pass it as a parameter from your markdown document if you want. (Of course, then you have to know what the URL is without having Hugo do it for you, which is not ideal.)</p>
<p>This approach has the disadvantages that you can't use the normal markdown heading syntax, and that you don't get Hugo's <a href="https://gohugo.io/extras/crossreferences/#what-about-duplicate-heading-anchors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">built-in resolution of duplicate anchors</a>. But it will get the job done.</p>

Q: Dropzone and asp.net core 3.1 - what are the proper param to be sent to controller? A: <p>TL;DR summary of @Xueli Chen's answer:</p>
<p>Frontend:</p>
<pre><code>function workaroundDropzone() {
 return &quot;files&quot;;
}

new Dropzone(&quot;div#yourId&quot;, { 
    paramName: workaroundDropzone,
    uploadMultiple: true
});
</code></pre>
<p>Backend:</p>
<pre><code>public async Task&lt;JsonResult&gt; DropzoneActionHandler(ICollection&lt;IFormFile&gt; files) {

}
</code></pre>
<p>Trick is in creating a method returning string with desired param name, Dropzone won't override it with <code>[]</code> as it would if <code>paramName</code> would be set to a string. Tested with version 5.2.7</p>
